#N canvas 0 0 720 520 10;
#X text 12 8 sn76489~ - TI SN76489 PSG (3 square tones + 1 LFSR noise);
#X text 12 28 The Mega Drive's second sound chip (next to the YM2612). Eats RAW register bytes - the vgm~ outlet-1 vocabulary \, so connect the two directly. A byte with bit 7 set latches channel/type AND carries 4 data bits. A byte with bit 7 clear completes a 10-bit tone period. 4-bit volumes: 0 = loudest \, 15 = silent (2 dB per step). Tone f = clock/(32*N). Power-up is SILENT (Sega integrated chip powers up muted) - write a volume to hear anything.;
#X floatatom 20 190 3 0 0 0 - - -;
#X text 70 190 one raw byte (0-255);
#X msg 20 230 142;
#X msg 70 230 15;
#X text 110 230 tone ch1 <- 0x0fe = 440.4 Hz (latch 0x8e \, data 0x0f);
#X msg 20 260 144;
#X text 110 260 volume ch1 <- 0x90 = 0 \, loudest;
#X msg 20 290 228;
#X msg 70 290 240;
#X text 110 290 noise: white \, rate 0 (0xe4) at full volume (0xf0);
#X msg 400 230 reset;
#X msg 400 260 clock 3579545;
#X text 400 290 also: psg <byte> \, gg <mask> \, gain <f>;
#X obj 20 340 sn76489~;
#X obj 20 380 *~ 0.5;
#X obj 20 420 dac~;
#X text 20 460 in a VGM patch: vgm~ outlet 1 -> sn76489~ inlet \, sn76489~ -> the mix (see the vgm-player example). vgm~ GG stereo (gg) and AY8910 (ay) writes arrive here too - ay is dropped silently.;
#X connect 3 0 14 0;
#X connect 4 0 14 0;
#X connect 6 0 14 0;
#X connect 8 0 14 0;
#X connect 9 0 14 0;
#X connect 11 0 14 0;
#X connect 12 0 14 0;
#X connect 14 0 15 0;
#X connect 15 0 16 0;
#X connect 15 0 16 1;
#X text 400 340 outlets (signal): 0 = the four channels summed \, 1..4 = tone 1-3 / noise alone - no internal clip (sum is full scale 1.0 at gain 1);
